    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 151101\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry weather and warming temperatures starting today, and lasting \nthrough Monday.\n\n- Unsettled weather will return Tuesday and again late week, leading \nto a couple periods with rain chances over south-central Arizona and \ncooling temperatures near to below daily normals.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nThe shortwave that moved across northern AZ yesterday is now \npositioned along the boarder between NM and TX. Behind the \nshortwave, to our region's west, upper level ridging is building and \nwill quickly move eastwards and strengthen over the Desert SW \nstarting today.That being said, H5 heights will climb back up to \nbetween 577-580 dam by this afternoon, which will diminish any \nfurther rainfall and begin to trend temperatures back into the mid \nto upper 70s across the lower deserts. Further warming will occur \nheading into Monday where temperatures will increase to the upper \n70s with the Phoenix Metro having near an 80% chance of hitting at \nleast 80F.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nBy late monday night/early Tuesday morning another upper level \ntrough will push inland with models now showing the core of this \nnext system lifting to the NE through NV and UT. However the bottom \nof the trough will move through Arizona providing enough ascent to \nthe region to increase PoPs back to between 60-90% across South-\nCentral AZ, and 20-60% across SW AZ. PWATs Tuesday will also \nincrease to over 200%, with the latest WPC QPF indicating Tuesday's \nrain totals between 0.25\"-0.50\" across the Greater Phoenix area, \nlocally higher in thunderstorms, and close to 1.00\" over Central AZ \nhigher terrain to the north/northeast. However, considerable spread \nremains in the model guidance, with 25th-75th percentile QPF ranging \nbetween a few tenths to upwards of 1.25\", focused over the higher \nterrain to the north/northeast of Phoenix. A 1-2 day break in the \nunsettled weather will then likely ensue following Tuesday, ahead of \nthe next system. \n\nDuring the latter half of the week, ensembles are pointing towards \nyet another upper level trough moving inland from the Pacific NW, \nhowever, there are still considerable discrepancies in the \ntrajectory of this followup system (how far south it will propagate, \nwhich will impact the moisture return and placement of the best \nupper level ascent mechanisms). Due to this, confidence remains low \nfor PoPs/QPF late in the work week, but at the very least, there is \nbuilding consensus for an even cooler airmass to enter the region as \na result of this second system. Latest NBM highs continue to trend \ntowards the 60s to lower 70s by late week. However, the spread in \nthe model guidance increases considerably on Friday, with IQRs on \nthe NBM forecast temperatures increasing to 8F-10F. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1100Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nPeriods of cigs near 6K ft AGL this morning and uncertainty in wind \ndirections will be the main weather issues through Monday morning. \nCloud decks in a 5K-7K ft AGL layer will gradually erode through the \nday, only clearing around sunset. Forecast confidence is good that \nlight east winds will prevail much of the period, however may become \nvariable or briefly obtain a W/NW trajectory during the later \nafternoon/early evening. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather issues will exist through Monday morning under \nslowly decreasing 5K-7K ft AGL cloud decks. Confidence in wind \ndirections is moderate favoring a W/SW component at KIPL, and light \nnorth winds becoming southerly at KBLH. Extended periods of nearly \ncalm conditions will be common. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry conditions and temperatures warming near to slightly above \nnormal are expected starting today lasting through Monday before \nanother weather system moves through the region on Tuesday. Despite \nthe upcoming drier and warmer conditions afternoon minimum \nhumidities will continue to range between 30-60% areawide through \nthe week, following good to excellent overnight recovery. Widespread \nbreezy and locally windy conditions in Southeast CA will develop \nwith the next system on Tuesday. This system will also cool \ntemperatures back into a below normal category. The next weather \nsystem will likely reach the area by Friday and result in even \ncooler temperatures and another round of rain chances. \n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area through at least early next week due to \nwater released from the dam. Water releases remain at 3000 cfs and \nare expected to remain at this level through the next several days. \nThis will have the potential to flood farm fields and low water \ncrossings along the river downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Whittock/Ryan \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock/Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero\n",
